title: 'pallet-xcm: weight asset claiming and precompile XCM input by their real size'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    Three related under-charges in XCM weighing, all exploitable to build blocks whose
    real execution cost far exceeds their accounted weight.

    **`claim_assets` extrinsic.** It was charged a flat weight benchmarked with a single
    asset, while a claim may contain up to `MAX_ITEMS_IN_ASSETS` (20) assets, each
    performing per-asset storage reads and writes through the `AssetTransactor`. The
    extrinsic is now annotated with a weight linear in the number of claimed assets and
    its benchmark is parameterized accordingly.

    Breaking change: `pallet_xcm::WeightInfo::claim_assets` now takes the number of
    assets as `n: u32`; chains with custom `pallet_xcm` weights must regenerate them.
    Chains whose `AssetTransactor` can deposit multiple distinct asset kinds (e.g. Asset
    Hub) must override the new (defaulted)
    `pallet_xcm::benchmarking::Config::get_assets` method so the benchmark measures a
    real per-asset weight slope; the default merges into a single asset entry and
    measures a flat weight, which is only sound for single-asset-kind transactors (e.g.
    relay chains). `VersionedAssets` gained `len()` and `is_empty()`.

    **`ClaimAsset` instruction.** Every runtime XCM weigher charged it a flat
    single-asset weight. They now multiply it by the number of assets in the
    instruction, as was already done for `WithdrawAsset`, `BurnAsset` and `ExpectAsset`.
    This also closes the remaining gap in the inner message built by the `claim_assets`
    extrinsic, whose `DepositAsset` half already scaled while its `ClaimAsset` half did
    not.

    **XCM precompile input.** The `pallet-revive` XCM precompile charged a flat weight
    for decoding and weighing a caller-supplied blob, so the only size-dependent cost
    was `pallet-revive`'s per-input-byte charge, which merely covers the calldata copy
    and ABI decode. `MAX_INSTRUCTIONS_TO_DECODE` does not bound the work, because the
    weigher fully decodes any call carried by a `Transact` and recurses
    `get_dispatch_info()` over it, and every `DoubleEncoded` boundary starts a fresh
    instruction and decode-depth budget. `weighMessage`, `execute` and `send` now charge
    for the size of the blob before decoding it; for `execute` this is a separate charge
    from the execution weight, which is reconciled down to the actual execution weight
    and would otherwise refund it.

    Breaking change: `pallet_xcm::WeightInfo::weigh_message` now takes the message
    length `n: u32`, and a new `decode_xcm(n)` measures decoding without weighing. The
    `weigh_message` benchmark builds its worst-case payload itself, needing only a
    batching call from the new `pallet_xcm::benchmarking::Config::batch_call`
    (implemented as `utility.batch` by all in-tree runtimes). Chains with a batching
    pallet that leave it at its `None` default measure a far cheaper slope and will
    under-charge the precompile.

    **`AliasOrigin` instruction.** Runtimes that put `pallet_xcm::AuthorizedAliasers` last
    in their `Aliasers` benchmarked the instruction with an origin/target pair matched by
    the first, storage-free filter (`AliasChildLocation`), so the weight covered none of
    the authorized-alias lookup. Their
    `pallet_xcm_benchmarks::generic::Config::alias_origin` now sets up the real worst
    case: a `target` account holding the maximum number of authorized aliasers, with the
    matching one registered last and carrying an expiry, so every cheaper filter is tried
    and the lookup iterates the full list and runs the expiration check. The shared setup
    lives in the new `parachains_common::benchmarking` module, gated on
    `runtime-benchmarks`, whose docs spell out the `Aliasers` configuration it assumes;
    chains configured differently have to work out their own worst case.
